://api.github.com/users/AKarmanov/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AKarmanov/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AKarmanov/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AKarmanov/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AKarmanov/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/AKarmanov","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/jcontent/commits/b8355cd9a117523e7fa54cb4eba5df1f389daf09/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-14T13:43:13Z","email":"alexandrk_@hotmail.com","name":"Alex Karmanov"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-14T13:43:13Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: Keep the page builder at the same scroll position when content is saved (#2665)\n\n* fix: Keep page builder on the same content when it reloads\n\nRestoring the scroll offset was the wrong idea. An offset only holds the\nview still while nothing above it changes height, and after a save plenty\ndoes: the content is a different size, images load late, and the\npage-builder decorations arrive with the boxes a few hundred milliseconds\nafter the document. Measured on the digitall home page, it comes back over\na thousand pixels shorter and inflates over the next second and a half. The\nnumber came back and the content did not — the highlight being edited ended\nup 334px above the top of the viewport, out of sight.\n\nRemember the view as content instead: which module, and how far below the\ntop edge it sat. Then put that module back at that distance and hold it\nthere while the reloaded document settles, so late-arriving layout moves the\npage under the anchor rather than moving the anchor.\n\nAnchor on the content being worked on, identified by the clicked element. An\nordinary Content Editor save reaches the page builder through\ntriggerRefetchAll and says nothing about what changed, and choosing by\ngeometry alone is not stable: nested modules compete for whichever sits\nnearest the top edge, and holding a nested module still lets its container\nmove — the same build measured 0px out on one run and 189px on the next.\n\nHold the anchor until the page goes quiet rather than for a fixed window: a\nheavy page settles in about a second and a half warm and longer cold, and\nstopping early is the defect. A hard cap covers a page that never settles,\nand any scrolling by the user ends it immediately — we never fight them for\nthe scrollbar. If the anchored content is gone from the reloaded document,\nfall back to the offset the editor had.\n\nAlso: style.top = ''-10000'' is invalid CSS, so hiding the outgoing frame was\na no-op and it kept covering the incoming one from the second swap on.\n\n* test: Cover the case that justifies anchoring on content\n\nWhen the layout above the viewport changes height, the same scroll offset\nframes different content. Measured at 884px of growth above the fold: the\nanchor holds the watched content exactly still by scrolling to compensate,\nwhich is precisely the error restoring the offset would have made. Any\noffset-based implementation fails this test, which is the point of it.\n\n* fix: Hold the page-builder anchor on a long page\n\nOn a long page the anchor either pinned the view to the top of the page or\nheld a position the editor was trying to change, for the best part of a\nminute. Two causes, both measured on digitall''s home page (55000px, 1332\nmodules).\n\nA module standing for content in general rather than one node renders\npath=\"*\", and there are 378 of them on that page. The geometry fallback\ncould pick one, and looking a path=\"*\" up in the reloaded document returns\nwhichever comes first — at 814px, near the top, with nothing to do with the\nmodule that was in view. The anchor then read as a 29000px drift and dragged\nthe editor to the top of the page: measured as a refresh at 30035px landing\nat 814px, on 106 of 216 scroll positions sampled across the page. Such a\npath is now never an anchor, and the raw offset covers the gap.\n\nThe quiet and cap windows were counted in ticks, which assumes the interval\nfires on schedule. It competes with the layout of the document it is\nwatching over one thread, so it does not: a 50ms interval fired every 806ms.\nThe 2s quiet window therefore lasted 32s and the 15s cap nearly 4 minutes.\nBoth are now read off the clock, and sized from what the page does — it\narrives 33060px tall, still grows at 11.4s, reaches 54836px at 14.9s, and\npauses as long as 3.6s mid-settle looking exactly like a page that has\nfinished.\n\nThe spec harness matched selectors by hand and so could not see the first\ndefect at all; queries now run against a real document.\n\n* fix: Finish the page-builder anchor''s edges, per review\n\nFive things review found around the anchor, none of them the main path but all\nof them reachable.\n\nThe content can go missing after the watch has started, and that was only half\nhandled. Whether a correction was called for stayed at its last answer once the\nanchor stopped answering, so nothing accumulated towards quiet and the only way\nout was the cap — half a minute of putting the editor back where they no longer\nare. The offset it falls back on was also fired once, and the reloaded document\nis far shorter than it ends up (33060px of an eventual 54836px on digitall''s\nhome page), so an offset further down than it can scroll to yet was clamped with\nnothing to come back to it. The watch now hands over to the offset and keeps\nreaching for it, and what feeds the quiet window is whether the view actually\nmoved rather than whether we asked it to — an ask nothing can honour yet is not\nthe page still settling. It still never re-anchors once the content has gone.\n\nOnly an absolute path identifies a node, so that is now the rule for what can be\nanchored on. It replaces the narrower \"not path=*\" and also covers a path\nrendered relative to its parent, which Boxes.jsx resolves before it uses one and\nwhich is no more unique in the document than * is. Not reproducible on digitall''s\nhome page — 0 of 1330 modules — so this closes it rather than fixes a live fault.\n\nDragging the scrollbar raises no wheel, touch or key event, so it was the one way\nof scrolling the watch would override. A press on the bar now steps out.\nDeliberately not a scroll listener diffed against the position we set: the\nbrowser moves the scroll by itself to keep content still as the page above it\ngrows, and reading that as the editor taking over would abandon the anchor on\nexactly the pages this is for.\n\nThe anchor also carries its horizontal position now, which the offset path\nalready restored.\n\nFinally, refresh() legitimately captures nothing when no module is in view, and a\nbare null could not be told from \"this was not a refresh\" — which sent the load\nhandler scanning a whole stale document again for the same answer.\n\nThe window harness gained scrollTo clamping at the top end, without which none of\nthe clamping above is observable in a test.\n\n* docs: Record what the anchor''s polling costs, and why it stands\n\nReview asked whether reading scrollHeight and getBoundingClientRect several times\na second, on the thread already laying out the document being watched, adds to the\ncontention the watch is waiting on. It does contend — the 50ms interval fired every\n806ms on digitall''s home page, and a 100ms sampler in the parent window stalled up\nto 3105ms, so it is the whole main thread — but whether it lengthens the settle is\nunmeasured, and loosening the interval on that hunch would trade a real behaviour\nfor a guess. Written down at the poll, with what would settle it and why a\nResizeObserver is not a drop-in replacement.\n\n* fix: Keep holding when the frame has nowhere to scroll yet\n\nA frame pinned at the end of a document that has not finished arriving cannot be\ncorrected at all: the position the anchor needs is further down than the document\ncan scroll to, so the ask lands short and nothing on screen moves. Taking movement\nas the sign that the page is still settling read that as settled and let go — a\nmoment before the rest of the page arrived and pushed the content away. It put the\nwatched content 132px below where it started on a refresh, which is what the frame\nposition spec caught once CI was slow enough for the document to still be growing\nwhen it measured.\n\nSo the ask now reports whether the frame could go where it was sent, and a clamped\nask keeps the watch alive alongside movement and height changes. It is the page\nowing us height rather than the page being still, and it cannot run away with the\nwatch: it can only hold while the document is too short, and the cap still ends it.","tree":{"sha":"e282a4d1c5a927eceaf7a3350a385108862e4539","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/jcontent/git/trees/e282a4d1c5a927eceaf7a3350a385108862e4539"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/jcontent/git/commits/b8355cd9a117523e7fa54cb4eba5df1f389daf09","verification":{"payload":"tree e282a4d1c5a927eceaf7a3350a385108862e4539\nparent 81606f9e008bacb35adec79dec0357a082733a1d\nauthor Alex Karmanov <alexandrk_@hotmail.com> 1786714993 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786714993 -0400\n\nfix: Keep the page builder at the same scroll position when content is saved (#2665)\n\n* fix: Keep page builder on the same content when it reloads\n\nRestoring the scroll offset was the wrong idea. An offset only holds the\nview still while nothing above it changes height, and after a save plenty\ndoes: the content is a different size, images load late, and the\npage-builder decorations arrive with the boxes a few hundred milliseconds\nafter the document. Measured on the digitall home page, it comes back over\na thousand pixels shorter and inflates over the next second and a half. The\nnumber came back and the content did not — the highlight being edited ended\nup 334px above the top of the viewport, out of sight.\n\nRemember the view as content instead: which module, and how far below the\ntop edge it sat. Then put that module back at that distance and hold it\nthere while the reloaded document settles, so late-arriving layout moves the\npage under the anchor rather than moving the anchor.\n\nAnchor on the content being worked on, identified by the clicked element. An\nordinary Content Editor save reaches the page builder through\ntriggerRefetchAll and says nothing about what changed, and choosing by\ngeometry alone is not stable: nested modules compete for whichever sits\nnearest the top edge, and holding a nested module still lets its container\nmove — the same build measured 0px out on one run and 189px on the next.\n\nHold the anchor until the page goes quiet rather than for a fixed window: a\nheavy page settles in about a second and a half warm and longer cold, and\nstopping early is the defect. A hard cap covers a page that never settles,\nand any scrolling by the user ends it immediately — we never fight them for\nthe scrollbar. If the anchored content is gone from the reloaded document,\nfall back to the offset the editor had.\n\nAlso: style.top = ''-10000'' is invalid CSS, so hiding the outgoing frame was\na no-op and it kept covering the incoming one from the second swap on.\n\n* test: Cover the case that justifies anchoring on content\n\nWhen the layout above the viewport changes height, the same scroll offset\nframes different content. Measured at 884px of growth above the fold: the\nanchor holds the watched content exactly still by scrolling to compensate,\nwhich is precisely the error restoring the offset would have made. Any\noffset-based implementation fails this test, which is the point of it.\n\n* fix: Hold the page-builder anchor on a long page\n\nOn a long page the anchor either pinned the view to the top of the page or\nheld a position the editor was trying to change, for the best part of a\nminute. Two causes, both measured on digitall''s home page (55000px, 1332\nmodules).\n\nA module standing for content in general rather than one node renders\npath=\"*\", and there are 378 of them on that page. The geometry fallback\ncould pick one, and looking a path=\"*\" up in the reloaded document returns\nwhichever comes first — at 814px, near the top, with nothing to do with the\nmodule that was in view. The anchor then read as a 29000px drift and dragged\nthe editor to the top of the page: measured as a refresh at 30035px landing\nat 814px, on 106 of 216 scroll positions sampled across the page. Such a\npath is now never an anchor, and the raw offset covers the gap.\n\nThe quiet and cap windows were counted in ticks, which assumes the interval\nfires on schedule. It competes with the layout of the document it is\nwatching over one thread, so it does not: a 50ms interval fired every 806ms.\nThe 2s quiet window therefore lasted 32s and the 15s cap nearly 4 minutes.\nBoth are now read off the clock, and sized from what the page does — it\narrives 33060px tall, still grows at 11.4s, reaches 54836px at 14.9s, and\npauses as long as 3.6s mid-settle looking exactly like a page that has\nfinished.\n\nThe spec harness matched selectors by hand and so could not see the first\ndefect at all; queries now run against a real document.\n\n* fix: Finish the page-builder anchor''s edges, per review\n\nFive things review found around the anchor, none of them the main path but all\nof them reachable.\n\nThe content can go missing after the watch has started, and that was only half\nhandled. Whether a correction was called for stayed at its last answer once the\nanchor stopped answering, so nothing accumulated towards quiet and the only way\nout was the cap — half a minute of putting the editor back where they no longer\nare. The offset it falls back on was also fired once, and the reloaded document\nis far shorter than it ends up (33060px of an eventual 54836px on digitall''s\nhome page), so an offset further down than it can scroll to yet was clamped with\nnothing to come back to it. The watch now hands over to the offset and keeps\nreaching for it, and what feeds the quiet window is whether the view actually\nmoved rather than whether we asked it to — an ask nothing can honour yet is not\nthe page still settling. It still never re-anchors once the content has gone.\n\nOnly an absolute path identifies a node, so that is now the rule for what can be\nanchored on. It replaces the narrower \"not path=*\" and also covers a path\nrendered relative to its parent, which Boxes.jsx resolves before it uses one and\nwhich is no more unique in the document than * is. Not reproducible on digitall''s\nhome page — 0 of 1330 modules — so this closes it rather than fixes a live fault.\n\nDragging the scrollbar raises no wheel, touch or key event, so it was the one way\nof scrolling the watch would override. A press on the bar now steps out.\nDeliberately not a scroll listener diffed against the position we set: the\nbrowser moves the scroll by itself to keep content still as the page above it\ngrows, and reading that as the editor taking over would abandon the anchor on\nexactly the pages this is for.\n\nThe anchor also carries its horizontal position now, which the offset path\nalready restored.\n\nFinally, refresh() legitimately captures nothing when no module is in view, and a\nbare null could not be told from \"this was not a refresh\" — which sent the load\nhandler scanning a whole stale document again for the same answer.\n\nThe window harness gained scrollTo clamping at the top end, without which none of\nthe clamping above is observable in a test.\n\n* docs: Record what the anchor''s polling costs, and why it stands\n\nReview asked whether reading scrollHeight and getBoundingClientRect several times\na second, on the thread already laying out the document being watched, adds to the\ncontention the watch is waiting on. It does contend — the 50ms interval fired every\n806ms on digitall''s home page, and a 100ms sampler in the parent window stalled up\nto 3105ms, so it is the whole main thread — but whether it lengthens the settle is\nunmeasured, and loosening the interval on that hunch would trade a real behaviour\nfor a guess. Written down at the poll, with what would settle it and why a\nResizeObserver is not a drop-in replacement.\n\n* fix: Keep holding when the frame has nowhere to scroll yet\n\nA frame pinned at the end of a document that has not finished arriving cannot be\ncorrected at all: the position the anchor needs is further down than the document\ncan scroll to, so the ask lands short and nothing on screen moves. Taking movement\nas the sign that the page is still settling read that as settled and let go — a\nmoment before the rest of the page arrived and pushed the content away. It put the\nwatched content 132px below where it started on a refresh, which is what the frame\nposition spec caught once CI was slow enough for the document to still be growing\nwhen it measured.\n\nSo the ask now reports whether the frame could go where it was sent, and a clamped\nask keeps the watch alive alongside movement and height changes. It is the page\nowing us height rather than the page being still, and it cannot run away with the\nwatch: it can only hold while the document is too short, and the cap still ends it.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfxtxCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAv4oQALEw/M0+C2XF0Q5i9bAYM/0R\n8AUc4PUq4WV2mZ2X3fYjpx27FOCWbcxJh37iKma3a21aHidgOlNYS/MMLvhsbc53\n7B4hqxO6ZcX7E+1i/lJ9XjA/1UG6s/LVFvrPeCAsropU85sEqLL+7CVIan8gS05X\nBfx1YV4zdbNfpcZ6uch21xazZ0SlUYIJOjMXkXf2Bhcd06D30x8cxGNGB+9YOUki\n+gsTF8JxkeNp1hSS3tnSjRhi6Aj0y/y2x8DYWiL9MABHbRTGr4p/04KdEpJSNXcK\nHaN0dgQyfscRxwEcwY5yqACZvJRXCo7H+b2/iYkRc6/pAW/OWLI54uU5w7PLW7As\nS+gKU8SoUPZVKYUAoWx8nSbFjyHMgywyv29j4FqMAojuGXZ3KW+VmmsJlDMfI7D6\nVn1GnL4MigE8Yuk03Pxt2u5ZmXzaQyrFtPz16WIhqnGkIF2a6duDXGFD/eEU9+1p